Certainly! Below is a Scala code snippet that creates a selection of 3 Italian-inspired appetizers, each from a different region, with the meats, cheeses, and vegetables all coming from the same region for each appetizer. I've chosen some well-known regions for their unique cured meats, aged cheeses, and marinated vegetables.

```scala
object Antipasto {
  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    // Define regions and their associated ingredients
    val regions = Map(
      "Emilia-Romagna" -> (List("Prosciutto di Parma", "Mascarpone", "Marinated Artichokes"),
                           List("Grana Padano", "Parmigiano-Reggiano", "Marinated Olives")),
      "Sicily" -> (List("Mortadella", "Caciocavallo", "Marinated Eggplants"),
                   List("Pecorino Siciliano", "Caciocavallo", "Marinated Fennel")),
      "Tuscany" -> (List("Bresaola", "Pecorino Toscano", "Marinated Mushrooms"),
                    List("Pecorino Toscano", "Pecorino Sardo", "Marinated Carrots"))
    )

    // Create the appetizers
    val appetizers = regions.map { case (region, (meats, cheeses, vegetables)) =>
      s"Appetizer from $region: " +
      s"Meat - ${meats.head}, Cheese - ${cheeses.head}, Vegetable - ${vegetables.head}"
    }

    // Print the appetizers in the required order
    appetizers.foreach(println)
  }
}
```

This code defines a map of regions to their associated cured meats, aged cheeses, and marinated vegetables. It then creates an appetizer for each region, ensuring that the meat, cheese, and vegetable all come from the same region, and prints them in the required order. You can expand the regions and their associated ingredients to include more variety or to match specific dietary requirements.
